2. The e of a name: closed /e/ when it ends the name, open /ɛ/ before a consonant
   bé         /be/        /e/ ends the name  as the rule says
   cé         /se/        /e/ ends the name  as the rule says
   dé         /de/        /e/ ends the name  as the rule says
   effe       /ɛf/        /ɛ/ before /f/     as the rule says
   gé         /ʒe/        /e/ ends the name  as the rule says
   elle       /ɛl/        /ɛ/ before /l/     as the rule says
   emme       /ɛm/        /ɛ/ before /m/     as the rule says
   enne       /ɛn/        /ɛ/ before /n/     as the rule says
   pé         /pe/        /e/ ends the name  as the rule says
   erre       /ɛʁ/        /ɛ/ before /ʁ/     as the rule says
   esse       /ɛs/        /ɛ/ before /s/     as the rule says
   té         /te/        /e/ ends the name  as the rule says
   vé         /ve/        /e/ ends the name  as the rule says
   double vé  /dubləve/   /e/ ends the name  as the rule says
   i grec     /iɡʁɛk/     /ɛ/ before /k/     as the rule says
   zède       /zɛd/       /ɛ/ before /d/     as the rule says
   16 e's in the names, 16 as the rule says, 0 exceptions

3. The shape of a consonant's name: by its Latin class, and by its French sound
   b  bé         vowel after   /b/ stop       fits         Latin stop       fits
   c  cé         vowel after   /s/ continuant does not fit Latin stop       fits
   d  dé         vowel after   /d/ stop       fits         Latin stop       fits
   f  effe       vowel before  /f/ continuant fits         Latin continuant fits
   g  gé         vowel after   /ʒ/ continuant does not fit Latin stop       fits
   h  ache       vowel before  /ʃ/ continuant fits         Latin -          
   j  ji         vowel after   /ʒ/ continuant does not fit Latin -          
   k  ka         vowel after   /k/ stop       fits         Latin stop       fits
   l  elle       vowel before  /l/ continuant fits         Latin continuant fits
   m  emme       vowel before  /m/ continuant fits         Latin continuant fits
   n  enne       vowel before  /n/ continuant fits         Latin continuant fits
   p  pé         vowel after   /p/ stop       fits         Latin stop       fits
   q  ku         vowel after   /k/ stop       fits         Latin stop       fits
   r  erre       vowel before  /ʁ/ continuant fits         Latin continuant fits
   s  esse       vowel before  /s/ continuant fits         Latin continuant fits
   t  té         vowel after   /t/ stop       fits         Latin stop       fits
   v  vé         vowel after   /v/ continuant does not fit Latin -          
   by the Latin class:  14 of 14 Latin consonant letters fit the rule
   by the French sound: 4 do not: c g j v

